$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\dfrac{x+3}{4-\sqrt{2x+22}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{x+3}{4-\sqrt{2x+22}}\cdot\dfrac{4+\sqrt{2x+22}}{4+\sqrt{2x+22}}\qquad\gray{\text{Rationalize the denominator}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{(x+3)(4+\sqrt{2x+22})}{4^2-(2x+22)}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{\cancel{(x+3)}(4+\sqrt{2x+22})}{-2\cancel{(x+3)}}\qquad\gray{\text{Cancel out common factors}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{4+\sqrt{2x+22}}{-2} \text{, for }x\neq -3
\end{align}$

This means that the two expressions have the same value for all $x$-values (in their domains) except for $-3$.

We can now use the following theorem:

If $f(x)=g(x)$ for all $x$-values in a given interval except for $x=c$, then $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to c}f(x)=\lim_{x\to c}g(x)$.
